// Class 12 Physics Unit III: Magnetic Effects of Current & Magnetism (Unit III: Magnetic Effects of Current & Magnetism)
// Comprehensive Deep Reference Textbook Standard (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora) + In-Depth Explanations + Real-World Intuitions + All 14 Diagrams

export const c12Phy3HtmlOverview = "<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #FFFFFF; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 16px;\">\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary &amp; Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p style=\"text-align: center; color: #94A3B8; font-size: 14px; margin: 0 0 14px 0;\">Essential Core Concepts &amp; Key Definitions &bull; Unit III: Magnetic Effects of Current &amp; Magnetism</p>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Magnetic Field (B):</b> Region around current/magnet where Lorentz force is exerted [Tesla (T), [M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]].\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Permeability (&mu;<sub>0</sub>):</b> Ability of vacuum to support magnetic lines: 4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> T m A<sup>-1</sup>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Lorentz Force:</b> Total force on moving charge: <b>F</b> = q(<b>E</b> + <b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>).\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Magnetic Dipole Moment:</b> Vector strength of current loop: <b>M</b> = N I <b>A</b> [A m<sup>2</sup> or J T<sup>-1</sup>].\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Bohr Magneton (&mu;<sub>B</sub>):</b> Quantum unit of atomic dipole moment: 9.274 &times; 10<sup>-24</sup> A m<sup>2</sup>.\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #00b09b;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Susceptibility (&chi;<sub>m</sub>):</b> Degree of magnetization induced in material: &mu;<sub>r</sub> = 1 + &chi;<sub>m</sub>.\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00b09b; border-bottom: 2px solid #00b09b; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">PART A: MOVING CHARGES AND MAGNETISM</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 20px;\">1. Concept of Magnetic Field &amp; Oersted's Experiment</h3>\n<p><b>Detailed Historical &amp; Physical Background:</b><br>Until the early 19th century, electricity (phenomena of static charges, sparks, and chemical batteries) and magnetism (natural lodestones, compass needles, and terrestrial navigation) were considered entirely distinct and unrelated natural phenomena. In the summer of 1820, Danish physicist <b>Hans Christian Oersted</b> made a revolutionary laboratory observation during a lecture demonstration. He noticed that whenever a switch was closed and an electric current flowed through a straight metallic wire, a magnetic compass needle placed parallel and directly beneath the wire instantly swung away from its natural geographic North-South equilibrium alignment. When the current was switched off, the needle returned to its original North-South position. Furthermore, reversing the direction of current in the wire caused the needle to deflect in the exact opposite direction.</p>\n<p><b>Physical Mechanism and Rules of Deflection:</b><br>1. <b>Origin of the Deflecting Force:</b> The flowing stream of mobile conduction electrons inside the wire establishes a circular magnetic field in the surrounding space. The magnetic compass needle, which is a tiny permanent magnetic dipole, experiences a mechanical torque that rotates it into alignment with the local magnetic field lines created by the wire.<br>2. <b>Ampere's Swimming Rule (SNOW Rule):</b> Imagine a swimmer swimming along the wire in the direction of electric current with their face turned towards the compass needle. The <b>North pole of the needle will always deflect towards the swimmer's left hand</b> (i.e. if current flows from <b>S</b>outh to <b>N</b>orth <b>O</b>ver the needle, the north pole deflects towards <b>W</b>est &ndash; <b>SNOW</b>).<br>3. <b>Right-Hand Thumb Rule:</b> If you grasp the current-carrying conductor in your right hand with the outstretched thumb pointing along current I, the curled fingers encircling the conductor indicate the circular direction of magnetic field lines <b>B</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Electricity &amp; Magnetism as Unified Forces</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">A stationary electric charge possesses only an electrostatic field (E-field) because its charge is at rest relative to the observer. However, the moment that charge begins to move (drift velocity in a current), it carries its electric field through space, which by relativistic transformation generates a magnetic field (B-field). Moving electricity is the fundamental physical origin of all magnetism!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Magnetic Field Intensity (B)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Magnetic field intensity at any point in space is defined as the force experienced per unit positive test charge moving with unit speed (1 m/s) in a direction perpendicular to the magnetic field: <b>F</b> = q (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>). [1 Tesla = 10<sup>4</sup> Gauss].</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Tesla (T) = N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup> = Wb m<sup>-2</sup></span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 1: Oersted's Experiment (Circuit &amp; Needle Deflection)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Oersted's Experiment (Circuit &amp; Needle Deflection)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 420px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Switch Off: No current flows &rarr; needle stays N-S. Switch On: Current flows &rarr; needle deflects immediately.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Static Charge Misconception</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Board exams frequently ask: 'What magnetic force is experienced by a stationary proton placed in a 5 Tesla magnetic field?' Answer: Exactly ZERO! A magnetic field exerts force ONLY on moving charges (F = q v B sin&theta; = 0 when v = 0).</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">2. Biot-Savart Law &amp; Axial Field of a Circular Loop</h3>\n<p><b>Theoretical Foundation &amp; Differential Formulation:</b><br>Following Oersted's discovery, French physicists <b>Jean-Baptiste Biot</b> and <b>F&eacute;lix Savart</b> performed precise quantitative experiments in 1820 to formulate the exact mathematical law governing how any arbitrary electric current distribution generates magnetic fields. Because steady current must flow in a continuous closed circuit, we cannot isolate an individual current element physically. However, mathematically, we divide any conductor into infinitesimal segments of vector length d<b>l</b> carrying current I, known as an <b>infinitesimal current element (I d<b>l</b>)</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Experimental Dependences of Biot-Savart Law:</b><br>The magnitude of the infinitesimal magnetic field d<b>B</b> produced by a current element I d<b>l</b> at an observation point P located at displacement vector <b>r</b> depends on four fundamental factors:<br>• Directly proportional to the electric current I (dB &prop; I).<br>• Directly proportional to the length of the current element dl (dB &prop; dl).<br>• Directly proportional to the sine of the angle &theta; between the element vector d<b>l</b> and displacement vector <b>r</b> (dB &prop; sin&theta;).<br>• Inversely proportional to the square of the distance r from the element to the point (dB &prop; 1/r<sup>2</sup> &ndash; inverse square law).<br>Combining these four experimental relations gives: <b>dB &prop; (I dl sin&theta;) / r<sup>2</sup></b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Biot-Savart Law in Vector Form (d<b>B</b>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>d<b>B</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I (d<b>l</b> &times; <b>r̂</b>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I (d<b>l</b> &times; <b>r</b>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b>, where &mu;<sub>0</sub> = 4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> T m A<sup>-1</sup> is the absolute magnetic permeability of free space.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Tesla (T)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 2: Biot-Savart Law - Circular Current Loop\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Biot-Savart Law - Circular Current Loop\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Field dB due to element dL at point P and center formula B = (&#956;&#8320; I) / (2 R).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Axial Magnetic Field of Circular Current Loop</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Physical Setup &amp; Geometry):</b> Consider a thin circular loop of radius R carrying a steady counter-clockwise current I, situated in the y-z plane with its center at origin O. We wish to calculate the total magnetic field <b>B</b> at an axial observation point P on the x-axis at distance x from the center O.<br><br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Infinitesimal Element Contribution):</b> Consider a small element d<b>l</b> at the top of the loop. The position vector from d<b>l</b> to point P is <b>r</b>, with magnitude r = &radic;(R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>). Because d<b>l</b> is in the y-z plane and <b>r</b> lies in the x-y plane, the angle between d<b>l</b> and <b>r̂</b> is strictly 90&deg; (sin 90&deg; = 1). Applying Biot-Savart law:<br>\n    dB = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I dl</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup></span></span>].<br><br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Resolution of Vectors &amp; Cancellation by Symmetry):</b> The field vector d<b>B</b> is perpendicular to the plane containing d<b>l</b> and <b>r</b>. Resolving d<b>B</b> into rectangular components:<br>\n    • Axial component along +x axis: dB<sub>x</sub> = dB sin&alpha;, where sin&alpha; = R / r = R / &radic;(R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>).<br>\n    • Perpendicular component: dB<sub>&perp;</sub> = dB cos&alpha;.<br>\n    For every current element d<b>l</b> on the upper half of the loop, there exists a diametrically opposite element on the bottom half whose perpendicular component dB<sub>&perp;</sub> points in the exact opposite direction. Hence, all perpendicular components integrate to zero (&Sigma; dB<sub>&perp;</sub> = 0).<br><br>\n    <b>Step 4 (Total Axial Integration for N turns):</b> The total magnetic field is obtained by integrating the axial components around the full circumference &oint; dl = 2&pi; R:<br>\n    B<sub>axial</sub> = &int; dB sin&alpha; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> &oint; dl = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I R (2&pi; R)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>.<br>\n    For a tightly wound circular coil of N turns:<br>\n    <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>B<sub>axial</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I R<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span></b></span>.<br><br>\n    <b>Important Boundary Limits:</b><br>\n    • <b>At the Center of the Loop (x = 0):</b> <b>B<sub>center</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 R</span></span></b>.<br>\n    • <b>At Large Distances (x &gt;&gt; R):</b> Denoting loop magnetic dipole moment M = N I A = N I (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>):<br>\n    <b>B<sub>axial</sub> &approx; (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x<sup>3</sup></span></span></b> (identical to electric dipole axial field with &mu;<sub>0</sub>/4&pi; replacing 1/4&pi;&epsilon;<sub>0</sub>).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 WORKED BOARD NUMERICAL #1</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>Problem:</b> A circular coil of 100 turns and radius 8.0 cm carries a steady current of 0.40 A. Calculate the magnitude of the magnetic field at the center of the coil.</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; color: #69F0AE; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Solution:</b><br>Given: N = 100, R = 0.08 m, I = 0.40 A.<br>Formula: B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I) / (2 R)<br>B = [(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 100 &times; 0.40] / [2 &times; 0.08] = [5.0265 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup>] / 0.16 = <b>3.14 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Tesla</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">3. Ampere's Circuital Law &amp; Applications (Straight Wire &amp; Solenoid)</h3>\n<p><b>Fundamental Principle &amp; Line Integral Concept:</b><br>While Biot-Savart law calculates magnetic field by summing element contributions, <b>Ampere's Circuital Law (ACL)</b> provides an exceptionally powerful integral method for calculating magnetic fields in systems possessing high geometric symmetry (such as straight cylindrical wires, infinite sheets, and cylindrical solenoids). It is the magnetic analogue of Gauss's Law in electrostatics.</p>\n<p>Ampere's Law states that the line integral of magnetic field vector <b>B</b> around any closed imaginary mathematical loop (called an <b>Amperian Loop</b>) is equal to exactly &mu;<sub>0</sub> times the total algebraic electric current enclosed by that loop, regardless of the loop's shape or size.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Ampere's Circuital Law (&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>enclosed</sub></b>, where the line integral is evaluated over a closed path encircling total net current I<sub>enclosed</sub>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Tesla-meter (T m)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 3: Ampere's Law &ndash; Infinitely Long Straight Wire\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Ampere's Law &ndash; Infinitely Long Straight Wire\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 420px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Concentric circular magnetic field loops around straight wire: B = (&#956;&#8320; I) / (2&#960; r).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Magnetic Field of an Infinitely Long Straight Wire via Ampere's Law</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>Step 1 (Symmetry &amp; Amperian Loop Choice):</b> Consider an infinitely long, straight thin wire carrying a steady current I. Due to cylindrical axial symmetry, the magnetic field lines form concentric circles centered on the wire.<br>\n    <b>Step 2 (Evaluating Line Integral):</b> We choose a circular Amperian loop of radius r concentric with the wire. At every point on this loop:<br>\n    • The magnitude B of the magnetic field is constant due to equal radial distance r.<br>\n    • The field vector <b>B</b> is directed tangentially along the loop, so the angle between <b>B</b> and line element d<b>l</b> is &theta; = 0&deg; (cos 0&deg; = 1).<br>\n    &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &oint; B dl cos 0&deg; = B &oint; dl = B (2&pi; r).<br>\n    <b>Step 3 (Applying Ampere's Law):</b><br>\n    B (2&pi; r) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>enclosed</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>B = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; r</span></span></b></span>.<br>\n    <b>Physical Meaning:</b> The magnetic field strength around a long straight wire decreases inversely with the first power of radial distance (B &prop; 1/r).\n  </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">4. Straight Solenoid (Qualitative &amp; Quantitative Treatment)</h3>\n<p><b>Construction and Internal Field Homogeneity:</b><br>A solenoid is an electrical device formed by closely winding an insulated conducting copper wire into a long helical cylinder of length L and radius R (where L &gt;&gt; R). When an electric current passes through the solenoid, each individual circular turn acts as a tiny magnetic dipole. In the interior space away from the ends, the axial magnetic field components of all adjacent turns add constructively while radial components cancel completely. This produces a remarkably uniform, strong, and parallel magnetic field inside the solenoid, while the magnetic field outside is practically zero.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Solenoid Magnetic Field (B)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Inside an ideal long solenoid: <b>B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I</b> (where n = N / L is the number of turns per unit length). At either extreme end of the solenoid: <b>B<sub>end</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I</b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Tesla (T)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 4: Straight Solenoid (Uniform Internal Magnetic Field)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Straight Solenoid (Uniform Internal Magnetic Field)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Helical turns produce a strong uniform internal magnetic field B = &#956;&#8320; n I.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 6px;\">📝 WORKED BOARD NUMERICAL #2</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>Problem:</b> A solenoid of length 0.5 m has 500 turns and carries current 2.5 A. Calculate the magnetic field (a) inside at the center, (b) at one end.</div>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px; color: #69F0AE; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b;\">Solution:</b><br>Turns per meter: n = N / L = 500 / 0.5 = 1000 turns/m.<br>(a) Center: B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 1000 &times; 2.5 = <b>3.14 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> Tesla</b>.<br>(b) End: B<sub>end</sub> = 1/2 B<sub>center</sub> = <b>1.57 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> Tesla</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">5. Force on a Moving Charge in Magnetic Field (Lorentz Force)</h3>\n<p><b>Lorentz Equation &amp; Physical Characteristics:</b><br>When a particle carrying electric charge q moves with velocity <b>v</b> in a region containing both an electric field <b>E</b> and a magnetic field <b>B</b>, the total net electromagnetic force acting on it is given by the comprehensive <b>Lorentz Force Equation</b>: <b>F</b> = q (<b>E</b> + <b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>). The magnetic component <b>F</b><sub>m</sub> = q (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) exhibits unique properties fundamental to modern particle accelerators and mass spectrometers.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Why Magnetic Force Does ZERO Work</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Imagine swinging a stone tied to a string in a circle. The string tension pulls inward (perpendicular to stone's movement), changing its direction but NEVER increasing its speed. Similarly, magnetic force is always 90&deg; to velocity (<b>F</b> &perp; <b>v</b>). Power P = <b>F</b> &sdot; <b>v</b> = F v cos 90&deg; = 0. Therefore, work done W = 0 and kinetic energy stays strictly constant!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Magnetic Lorentz Force (<b>F</b><sub>m</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b><b>F</b><sub>m</sub> = q (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Magnitude: F = q v B sin &theta;. Force is maximum when &theta; = 90&deg; (F = q v B) and zero when &theta; = 0&deg; or 180&deg;. Direction given by Fleming's Left-Hand Rule or Right-Hand Palm Rule.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Newton (N)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 5: Force on Moving Charge &amp; Trajectory Path\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Force on Moving Charge &amp; Trajectory Path\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 600px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Lorentz force F = q(v &times; B) producing circular trajectory with Right-Hand Rule.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px;\">Detailed Breakdown: Trajectories of Charged Particles in Uniform Magnetic Field</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n      <li><b>1. Straight Line Path (&theta; = 0&deg; or 180&deg;):</b> When a charged particle moves parallel or anti-parallel to magnetic field lines, F<sub>m</sub> = q v B sin 0&deg; = 0. The particle continues in uniform straight-line motion unaffected.</li>\n      <li><b>2. Circular Orbit (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> When entering perpendicular to <b>B</b>, the magnetic force acts as a centripetal force: (m v<sup>2</sup> / r) = q v B.<br>\n      • <b>Orbital Radius:</b> <b>r = (m v) / (q B) = p / (q B) = &radic;(2 m K) / (q B)</b>.<br>\n      • <b>Period of Revolution:</b> <b>T = 2&pi; r / v = (2&pi; m) / (q B)</b>.<br>\n      • <b>Cyclotron Frequency:</b> <b>f = 1 / T = (q B) / (2&pi; m)</b>.<br>\n      <i>Crucial Fact:</i> Both time period T and frequency f are completely independent of speed v and orbit radius r!</li>\n      <li><b>3. Helical Path (0 &lt; &theta; &lt; 90&deg;):</b> When velocity makes an acute angle &theta; with <b>B</b>, velocity resolves into:<br>\n      • Parallel component v<sub>||</sub> = v cos&theta; (constant translational motion along <b>B</b>).<br>\n      • Perpendicular component v<sub>&perp;</sub> = v sin&theta; (circular orbit perpendicular to <b>B</b> with radius r = (m v sin&theta;) / (q B)).<br>\n      The combined motion is a <b>Helix</b> with linear pitch <b>p = v<sub>||</sub> T = (2&pi; m v cos&theta;) / (q B)</b>.</li>\n      <li><b>4. Velocity Selector (Wien Filter):</b> When crossed electric (<b>E</b>) and magnetic (<b>B</b>) fields are applied such that electric force F<sub>e</sub> = q E and magnetic force F<sub>m</sub> = q v B act in opposite directions, the forces balance when q E = q v B &rArr; <b>v = E / B</b>. Particles with this exact velocity pass straight through undeflected.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">6. Force Between Two Parallel Conductors &amp; Definition of 1 Ampere</h3>\n<p><b>Mechanism of Mutual Magnetic Interaction:</b><br>Consider two long, straight parallel conductors 1 and 2 separated by distance d in vacuum, carrying steady currents I<sub>1</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>. Wire 1 produces a cylindrical magnetic field B<sub>1</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub>) / (2&pi; d) throughout space. Wire 2 carrying current I<sub>2</sub> sits in this external field B<sub>1</sub> and experiences magnetic force <b>F</b><sub>21</sub> = I<sub>2</sub> (<b>L</b> &times; <b>B</b><sub>1</sub>). By Fleming's Left-Hand Rule, if currents flow in the <b>same direction, the force pulls Wire 2 towards Wire 1 (Attractive)</b>. By Newton's third law, Wire 1 experiences an equal and opposite attractive force <b>F</b><sub>12</sub> towards Wire 2.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Parallel vs Opposite Currents</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Unlike static electric charges where like charges repel, in magnetism <b>like (parallel) currents attract</b> each other, while <b>opposite (antiparallel) currents repel</b> each other!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Force per Unit Length (F / L)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; d</span></span></b> [N m<sup>-1</sup>].</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Newton per meter (N m<sup>-1</sup>)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 6: Force between Parallel Conductors (1 Ampere Definition)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Force between Parallel Conductors (1 Ampere Definition)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 600px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Parallel currents attract with F/L = (&#956;&#8320; I&#8321; I&#8322;) / (2&#960; d), defining standard SI 1 Ampere.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.08); border: 1.2px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Standard SI Definition of One Ampere (1 A):</b>\n    <p style=\"margin: 6px 0 0 0; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\">One Ampere is the constant current which, if maintained in two straight parallel conductors of infinite length and negligible circular cross-section placed 1 meter apart in vacuum, produces between these conductors a force equal to exactly <b>2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> Newton per meter of length</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">7. Moving Coil Galvanometer (MCG) Structure &amp; Radial Field</h3>\n<p><b>Principle, Construction &amp; Working Mechanism:</b><br>A Moving Coil Galvanometer is an electromagnetic instrument used for detecting and measuring minute electric currents. It operates on the principle that a current-carrying coil suspended in a uniform magnetic field experiences a mechanical deflecting torque &tau; = N I A B sin&theta;.</p>\n<p><b>Key Structural Components:</b><br>1. <b>Concave Magnetic Poles:</b> Cylindrical concave north and south pole pieces create a <b>Radial Magnetic Field</b>.<br>2. <b>Soft Iron Core:</b> A soft iron cylinder placed inside the coil concentrates magnetic flux lines and ensures the magnetic field is strictly radial in the gap.<br>3. <b>Phosphor-Bronze Strip &amp; Hairsprings:</b> Provide restoring torque per unit twist (k) with high tensile strength and minimal elastic fatigue.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Current & Voltage Sensitivity (I<sub>s</sub>, V<sub>s</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Current Sensitivity: <b>I<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&phi;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span></span></b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Voltage Sensitivity: <b>V<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&phi;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k R<sub>g</sub></span></span></b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">div A<sup>-1</sup>, div V<sup>-1</sup></span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[A<sup>-1</sup>], [V<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 7: Moving Coil Galvanometer (MCG) Structure\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Moving Coil Galvanometer (MCG) Structure\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 420px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Concave magnetic pole pieces and cylindrical soft iron core produce a strictly radial magnetic field.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.5); border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); padding-bottom: 6px;\">📐 STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL DERIVATION: Working Theory of Moving Coil Galvanometer</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75;\">\n    <b>1. Deflecting Torque:</b> In a radial magnetic field, the normal to the coil is always perpendicular to <b>B</b> (&theta; = 90&deg;). Thus, deflecting torque is constantly maximum:<br>\n    &tau;<sub>d</sub> = N I A B sin 90&deg; = N I A B.<br>\n    <b>2. Restoring Torque:</b> As the coil rotates through angle &phi;, the phosphor-bronze suspension strip twists, producing restoring torque:<br>\n    &tau;<sub>r</sub> = k &phi; (where k is torsional restoring couple per unit twist).<br>\n    <b>3. Dynamic Equilibrium:</b> Equating deflecting torque to restoring torque (&tau;<sub>d</sub> = &tau;<sub>r</sub>):<br>\n    N I A B = k &phi; &rArr; <span style=\"color: #76FF03; font-family: monospace; font-size: 16px;\"><b>I = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span></span>) &phi; = G<sub>0</sub> &phi;</b></span>.<br>\n    Since k, N, A, B are fixed geometric constants, deflection is strictly directly proportional to current: <b>&phi; &prop; I</b>, producing a perfectly linear scale!\n  </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(220, 38, 38, 0.1); border: 1.2px solid rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM TRAP &amp; CONCEPTUAL PITFALL: Doubling Turns Sensitivity Trap</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FCA5A5; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Doubling the turns of the galvanometer coil (N &rarr; 2N) doubles current sensitivity I_s. However, doubling turns doubles the wire length and hence coil resistance (R_g &rarr; 2R_g). Therefore, voltage sensitivity V_s = I_s / R_g = (2 I_s) / (2 R_g) = I_s / R_g remains completely UNCHANGED!</div>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">8. Galvanometer Conversions (As Ammeter &amp; As Voltmeter)</h3>\n<p><b>Conversion Theory &amp; Circuit Modifications:</b><br>A basic galvanometer is a very delicate, low-current instrument (full-scale deflection current I<sub>g</sub> is typically 1 mA to 10 mA, resistance G &approx; 10 &Omega; to 100 &Omega;). To measure large currents (e.g. 0 to 10 A) or high voltages (e.g. 0 to 100 V), we modify its circuit configuration.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7); border: 1.2px dashed #38BDF8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 14px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #38BDF8; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">💡 REAL-WORLD INTUITION: Ammeter Shunt Bypass Analogy</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Think of an ammeter shunt as a wide multi-lane bypass highway. When a massive flood of current (e.g. 10 Amperes) arrives at the meter, 99.9% flows safely through the low-resistance shunt bypass (S), while only a tiny safe trickle (I_g = 0.001 A) passes through the delicate galvanometer coil, protecting it from burning!</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Conversion Formulas (S, R)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>Ammeter (Low Shunt in Parallel):</b> S = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub> G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I - I<sub>g</sub></span></span> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Voltmeter (High Resistor in Series):</b> R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub></span></span> - G.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Ohm (&Omega;)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-3</sup> A<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 8: Galvanometer Conversions (As Ammeter &amp; As Voltmeter)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Galvanometer Conversions (As Ammeter &amp; As Voltmeter)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 460px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Left: Shunt S in parallel creates Ammeter. Right: High resistance R in series creates Voltmeter.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Summary Comparison: Ammeter vs Voltmeter</b>\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <tr style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.2);\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Feature</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Ammeter</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Voltmeter</th></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Measures</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Electric Current (Amperes)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Potential Difference (Volts)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Connected In</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>Series</b> in circuit branch</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>Parallel</b> across components</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Added Resistor</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Low resistance Shunt (S) in parallel</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">High resistance (R) in series</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Ideal Resistance</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>R<sub>A</sub> = 0 &Omega; (Zero)</b></td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\"><b>R<sub>V</sub> = &infin; (Infinite)</b></td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00b09b; border-bottom: 2px solid #00b09b; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px;\">PART B: MAGNETISM AND MATTER</h2>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 20px;\">9. Bar Magnet &amp; Continuous Magnetic Field Lines</h3>\n<p><b>Physical Concept &amp; Dipolar Nature:</b><br>A permanent bar magnet is the quintessential magnetic dipole, consisting of two fictitious magnetic poles of equal and opposite pole strength (+m and -m) separated by magnetic length 2l (where 2l &approx; 0.84 &times; geometric length). The vector magnetic dipole moment <b>M</b> points internally from South pole to North pole (<b>M</b> = m &times; 2<b>l</b>).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Gauss's Law for Magnetism (&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">The total net magnetic flux passing through ANY closed Gaussian surface is strictly ZERO: <b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = 0</b>. This fundamental law proves that isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist in nature; magnetic lines are continuous endless loops.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Weber (Wb)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 9: Bar Magnet &amp; Continuous Closed Field Lines\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Bar Magnet &amp; Continuous Closed Field Lines\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 460px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Continuous closed loops running N &rarr; S outside and S &rarr; N inside (&oint; B &sdot; dA = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Fundamental Properties of Magnetic Field Lines:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>1. <b>Continuous Closed Loops:</b> Magnetic field lines emerge from the North pole, curve through space, enter the South pole externally, and continue from South to North internally without breaking. (Contrast: Electric field lines start at +q and terminate at -q).</li>\n      <li>2. <b>Tangent Gives Direction:</b> The tangent drawn to a magnetic field line at any point gives the unique direction of resultant magnetic field <b>B</b> at that spatial position.</li>\n      <li>3. <b>Non-Intersection Principle:</b> Two magnetic field lines can never cross or intersect. If they did, two tangents could be drawn at the point of intersection, implying two distinct directions of resultant magnetic field, which is physical nonsense.</li>\n      <li>4. <b>Field Strength &amp; Line Density:</b> Relative density of field lines represents field magnitude (crowded near pole tips where field is strongest).</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">10. Bar Magnet as an Equivalent Solenoid Comparison</h3>\n<p><b>Equivalence Proof &amp; Atomic Amperean Currents:</b><br>Ampere proposed that all magnetism is produced by microscopic atomic circulating currents. A finite current-carrying solenoid of length 2l and radius a with n turns per unit length carrying current I produces an external magnetic field configuration strictly identical to a permanent bar magnet of dipole moment <b>M = N I A = (n &middot; 2l) I (&pi; a<sup>2</sup>)</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Magnetic Dipole Moment of Solenoid (M)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>M = N I A</b> = (n L) I (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>). Vector points from South pole to North pole.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Ampere-meter<sup>2</sup> (A m<sup>2</sup>)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 10: Bar Magnet as an Equivalent Solenoid\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Bar Magnet as an Equivalent Solenoid\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 460px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Side-by-side comparison showing identical external dipolar magnetic field patterns.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">11. Magnetic Field Intensity on Axis &amp; Equator of Dipole</h3>\n<p><b>Comparative Field Analysis:</b><br>Just as an electric dipole has axial and equatorial field formulas, a magnetic dipole of magnetic length 2l and dipole moment M produces field intensities characterized by inverse-cube distance attenuation (1/r<sup>3</sup>) for distances r &gt;&gt; l.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Axial & Equatorial Fields (B<sub>axial</sub>, B<sub>eq</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><b>Axial Field (End-on position):</b> <b>B<sub>axial</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b> (along M).<br><b>Equatorial Field (Broadside-on position):</b> <b>B<sub>eq</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b> (opposite to M).<br>Ratio at same distance: <b>B<sub>axial</sub> : B<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Tesla (T)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 11: Field Intensity on Axial &amp; Equatorial Lines\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Field Intensity on Axial &amp; Equatorial Lines\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 460px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 B_axial along M vs B_eq opposite to M with 2 : 1 ratio at distance r.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">12. Torque on a Magnetic Dipole in Uniform Magnetic Field</h3>\n<p><b>Mechanical Couple &amp; Potential Energy Derivation:</b><br>When a magnetic dipole (compass needle or bar magnet) of moment <b>M</b> is placed at angle &theta; in a uniform magnetic field <b>B</b>, equal and opposite forces (F = m B) act on North and South poles. While net translational force is zero (<b>F</b><sub>net</sub> = 0), these forces form a couple of arm 2l sin&theta;, exerting deflecting torque: <b>&tau; = <b>M</b> &times; <b>B</b></b> (magnitude &tau; = M B sin&theta;).</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Dipole Torque & Potential Energy (&tau;, U)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Torque: <b>&tau; = <b>M</b> &times; <b>B</b> = M B sin &theta;</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Potential Energy: <b>U = - <b>M</b> &sdot; <b>B</b> = - M B cos &theta;</b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">N m, Joules (J)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>2</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 12: Torque on a Magnetic Dipole in Uniform Field\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Torque on a Magnetic Dipole in Uniform Field\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 440px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Deflecting couple torque &tau; = M B sin&theta; aligning dipole with external field.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Equilibrium Orientations of a Magnetic Dipole:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>• <b>Stable Equilibrium (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b> Torque &tau; = 0, Potential Energy <b>U<sub>min</sub> = - M B</b>. Dipole aligns in the direction of external field <b>B</b>.</li>\n      <li>• <b>Zero Potential Energy Orientation (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> Torque &tau; = M B (Maximum twist), <b>U = 0</b>.</li>\n      <li>• <b>Unstable Equilibrium (&theta; = 180&deg;):</b> Torque &tau; = 0, Potential Energy <b>U<sub>max</sub> = + M B</b>. Dipole points antiparallel to <b>B</b>; any tiny perturbation causes it to flip 180&deg;.</li>\n      <li>• <b>Work Done in Rotating Dipole from &theta;<sub>1</sub> to &theta;<sub>2</sub>:</b> <b>W = - M B (cos&theta;<sub>2</sub> - cos&theta;<sub>1</sub>)</b>.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">13. Magnetic Properties of Materials (Dia-, Para-, Ferro-)</h3>\n<p><b>Microscopic Electronic Origin of Magnetism:</b><br>All macroscopic materials placed in an external magnetic field exhibit magnetic responses categorized into three primary classes based on electron orbital motions, electron spin alignments, and quantum domain structures:</p>\n<p>1. <b>Diamagnetism:</b> Present in all materials with paired electrons (zero net permanent atomic dipole moment). In an applied external field, orbital electron speeds change such that induced magnetic moments oppose the external field according to <b>Lenz's Law</b>, causing feeble repulsion independent of temperature.<br>2. <b>Paramagnetism:</b> Arises in atoms having permanent dipole moments due to unpaired electrons. Thermal agitation normally randomizes dipole orientations. An applied field feebly aligns dipoles along <b>B</b>, obeying <b>Curie's Law: &chi;<sub>m</sub> = C / T</b>.<br>3. <b>Ferromagnetism:</b> Arises from quantum mechanical exchange coupling which forces neighbouring atomic dipoles to align spontaneously in microscopic regions (~1 mm<sup>3</sup>) called <b>magnetic domains</b> (~10<sup>11</sup> atoms). An applied field expands and rotates aligned domains, creating intense net magnetization.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Magnetic Susceptibility & Permeability (&chi;<sub>m</sub>, &mu;<sub>r</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">Susceptibility: <b>&chi;<sub>m</sub> = M / H</b> (ratio of induced magnetization M to applied magnetic intensity H). Relative Permeability: <b>&mu;<sub>r</sub> = 1 + &chi;<sub>m</sub></b>.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Dimensionless</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>0</sup> A<sup>0</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 13: Magnetic Properties of Materials (Dia, Para, Ferro)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Magnetic Properties of Materials (Dia, Para, Ferro)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 600px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Dia: Field lines expelled | Para: Field lines feebly attracted | Ferro: Field lines strongly concentrated.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Master Comparison Matrix: Dia vs Para vs Ferro</b>\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 10px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <tr style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.2);\"><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Property</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Diamagnetic</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Paramagnetic</th><th style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Ferromagnetic</th></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">External Field Behavior</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Feebly repelled (pushed away)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Feebly attracted (pulled in)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Strongly pulled &amp; trapped</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Field Line Distortion</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Expelled from sample</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Feebly concentrated inside</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Intensely crowded through domains</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Susceptibility (&chi;<sub>m</sub>)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Negative &amp; small (-10<sup>-5</sup>)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Positive &amp; small (+10<sup>-4</sup>)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Positive &amp; very large (&gt; 1000)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Relative Permeability (&mu;<sub>r</sub>)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">&mu;<sub>r</sub> &lt; 1 (0.9999)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">&mu;<sub>r</sub> &gt; 1 (1.0001)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">&mu;<sub>r</sub> &gt;&gt; 1000</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Temperature Effect</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Independent of temperature</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">&chi; &prop; 1/T (Curie's Law)</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Becomes Para above T<sub>c</sub></td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Hysteresis Effect</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Zero hysteresis</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Zero hysteresis</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Exhibits B-H Hysteresis Loop</td></tr>\n      <tr><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Representative Examples</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Bismuth, Copper, Water, Gold</td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Aluminium, Sodium, Liquid O<sub>2</sub></td><td style=\"padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #334155;\">Iron, Cobalt, Nickel, Alnico, Steel</td></tr>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n<h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 30px;\">14. Magnetization vs Temperature &amp; Curie Temperature (T<sub>c</sub>)</h3>\n<p><b>Domain Disruption &amp; Phase Transition:</b><br>In ferromagnetic substances, atomic magnetic dipoles within each domain remain parallelly locked due to quantum mechanical exchange forces. As temperature increases, thermal kinetic agitation causes atomic lattice vibrations. At a specific critical transition temperature termed the <b>Curie Temperature (T<sub>c</sub>)</b>, thermal energy overcomes the exchange interaction, completely dismantling domain structures. Above T<sub>c</sub>, the material transforms from ferromagnetic into a standard paramagnetic substance obeying the <b>Curie-Weiss Law</b>.</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Curie Temperature & Curie-Weiss Law (T<sub>c</sub>, &chi;<sub>m</sub>)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 8px;\">The temperature above which a ferromagnetic material loses ferromagnetism and becomes paramagnetic: <b>&chi;<sub>m</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T - T<sub>c</sub></span></span></b> (for T &gt; T<sub>c</sub>).</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Kelvin (K)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b>Dimension:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[K]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); border: 1.8px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin: 24px 0; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25);\">\n    <div style=\"color: #00b09b; font-weight: bold; font-size: 17px; margin-bottom: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.3px;\">\n      📍 Diagram 14: Magnetization vs Temperature &amp; Curie Point (T_c)\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; padding: 6px 0;\">\n      <img src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"Magnetization vs Temperature &amp; Curie Point (T_c)\" style=\"max-width: 100%; width: 480px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 18px rgba(0,0,0,0.4); background: #FFFFFF; display: block;\" />\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"color: #CBD5E1; font-size: 14.5px; margin-top: 12px; font-weight: 500; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      💡 Transition of ferromagnetic to paramagnetic substance at Curie temperature T_c.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.7); border: 1px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.3); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <b style=\"color: #00b09b; font-size: 15.5px;\">Curie Temperatures of Standard Magnetic Elements:</b>\n    <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n      <li>• <b>Iron (Fe):</b> T<sub>c</sub> = 1043 K (770 &deg;C)</li>\n      <li>• <b>Cobalt (Co):</b> T<sub>c</sub> = 1394 K (1121 &deg;C) &ndash; Highest Curie temperature!</li>\n      <li>• <b>Nickel (Ni):</b> T<sub>c</sub> = 631 K (358 &deg;C)</li>\n      <li>• <b>Gadolinium (Gd):</b> T<sub>c</sub> = 289 K (16 &deg;C) &ndash; Becomes paramagnetic at normal room temperature!</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.08); border: 1.5px solid #00b09b; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px; margin-top: 35px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin-top: 0; font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet</h3>\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; margin-top: 14px; font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Biot-Savart:</b> dB = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> / 4&pi;) [I dl sin&theta; / r<sup>2</sup>]</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Coil Center:</b> B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I) / (2 R)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Straight Wire:</b> B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I) / (2&pi; r)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Solenoid Inside:</b> B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Orbit Radius:</b> r = (m v) / (q B)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Parallel Wires:</b> F/L = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub>) / (2&pi; d)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Galv. Shunt:</b> S = (I<sub>g</sub> G) / (I - I<sub>g</sub>)</div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(15,23,42,0.8); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 6px;\"><b>Axial / Eq:</b> B<sub>axial</sub> : B<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

export const c12Phy3HtmlSolutions = "<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #FFFFFF; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n<h2 style=\"color: #00b09b; border-bottom: 2px solid #00b09b; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">SECTION A: Very Short Answer Questions (1 Mark Each) — Q1 to Q40</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q1: What did Oersted's historic experiment prove regarding electric currents?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Oersted's experiment proved that electric currents produce magnetic fields in the surrounding space, establishing the fundamental unified connection between electricity and magnetism.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q2: Define magnetic field intensity (B) and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Magnetic field intensity (<b>B</b>) is the force experienced per unit positive test charge moving with unit speed perpendicular to the field: <b>F</b> = q (<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>). Its SI unit is the <b>Tesla (T)</b> [1 T = 1 N A<sup>-1</sup> m<sup>-1</sup> = 10<sup>4</sup> Gauss].</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q3: State the dimensional formula of magnetic field intensity (B).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[B] = [F] / ([q] [v]) = [M L T<sup>-2</sup>] / ([A T] [L T<sup>-1</sup>]) = <b>[M<sup>1</sup> L<sup>0</sup> T<sup>-2</sup> A<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q4: State Biot-Savart law in vector mathematical notation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>d<b>B</b> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I (d<b>l</b> &times; <b>r̂</b>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I (d<b>l</b> &times; <b>r</b>)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b>, where &mu;<sub>0</sub> = 4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> T m A<sup>-1</sup>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q5: Write the formula for magnetic field at the center of a circular coil of N turns carrying current I.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>B<sub>center</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 R</span></span></b>, directed perpendicular to the plane of the coil along the Right-Hand Thumb Rule.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q6: State the Right-Hand Thumb Rule for finding magnetic field around a straight current-carrying wire.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Imagine holding the wire in your right hand with the outstretched thumb pointing along current I. The curled fingers encircling the wire give the direction of circular magnetic field lines.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q7: State Ampere's Circuital Law in mathematical form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>enclosed</sub></b>, stating that the line integral of magnetic field around any closed loop equals &mu;<sub>0</sub> times total enclosed current.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q8: Write the expression for magnetic field inside a long ideal straight solenoid.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I</b>, where n = N / L is the number of turns per unit length and I is the current.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q9: What is the magnetic field at the extreme end of a long semi-infinite solenoid?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>B<sub>end</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I</b> (exactly half of the central magnetic field value).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q10: Why is the work done by a static magnetic force on a moving charged particle always zero?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Because magnetic force <b>F</b><sub>m</sub> = q(<b>v</b> &times; <b>B</b>) is always perpendicular to velocity <b>v</b> (power P = <b>F</b> &sdot; <b>v</b> = 0). Thus, speed and kinetic energy remain constant.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q11: Under what condition does a charged particle pass through a magnetic field without experiencing any magnetic force?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">When the particle travels <b>parallel or antiparallel</b> to the magnetic field lines (&theta; = 0&deg; or 180&deg;), giving F = q v B sin 0&deg; = 0.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q12: What is the trajectory of a charged particle entering a uniform magnetic field at an angle &theta; = 90&deg;?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The trajectory is a <b>uniform circle (circular path)</b> in the plane perpendicular to <b>B</b> with radius r = (m v) / (q B).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q13: What is the trajectory of a charged particle entering a uniform magnetic field at an arbitrary angle &theta; (0 < &theta; < 90&deg;)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The trajectory is a <b>Helix (helical path)</b> with radius r = (m v sin &theta;) / (q B) and pitch p = (2&pi; m v cos &theta;) / (q B).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q14: Write the formula for the radius of a circular orbit of a charged particle in a magnetic field in terms of momentum p.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">p</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(2 m K)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q15: Why is cyclotron frequency independent of particle speed and orbit radius?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Because frequency f = 1/T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; m</span></span>. As speed v increases, radius r increases in exact direct proportion (r &prop; v), keeping the orbital time period T constant.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q16: State the condition for an undeviated beam of charged particles in a velocity selector.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Crossed electric and magnetic fields (<b>E</b> &perp; <b>B</b>) produce balanced opposing forces (q E = q v B) when <b>v = E / B</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q17: Write the formula for magnetic force on a current-carrying conductor of length L.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b><b>F</b> = I (<b>L</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Magnitude: F = I L B sin &theta;, with direction given by Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q18: State Fleming's Left-Hand Rule.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Stretch thumb, forefinger, and middle finger mutually perpendicular: <b>Forefinger</b> &rarr; Magnetic Field (<b>B</b>), <b>Middle finger</b> &rarr; Current (I), then <b>Thumb</b> points in direction of <b>Force (Motion)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q19: Write the expression for force per unit length between two parallel current-carrying wires separated by distance d.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; d</span></span> [N m<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q20: Do two parallel wires carrying currents in opposite directions attract or repel?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">They <b>repel</b> each other (parallel currents in the same direction attract).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q21: State the official SI definition of One Ampere (1 A).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">One Ampere is the constant current which, when flowing through two parallel infinitely long straight wires 1 meter apart in vacuum, produces a mutual force of <b>2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> N m<sup>-1</sup></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q22: Write the formula for torque experienced by a planar current loop of N turns in a magnetic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b><b>&tau;</b> = <b>M</b> &times; <b>B</b> = N I (<b>A</b> &times; <b>B</b>)</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Magnitude: &tau; = N I A B sin &theta;.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q23: Define magnetic dipole moment of a planar current loop and state its SI unit.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Magnetic dipole moment is the vector product of current and loop area: <b><b>M</b> = N I <b>A</b></b>. SI Unit: <b>Ampere-meter<sup>2</sup> (A m<sup>2</sup>) or J T<sup>-1</sup></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q24: What is the value and formula of the Bohr Magneton (&mu;<sub>B</sub>)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>&mu;<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e &hbar;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 m<sub>e</sub></span></span> = 9.274 &times; 10<sup>-24</sup> A m<sup>2</sup></b>, the fundamental quantum of atomic magnetic dipole moment.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q25: What is the function of a radial magnetic field in a moving coil galvanometer?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">It ensures that the normal to the coil is always perpendicular to <b>B</b> (&theta; = 90&deg;), making deflecting torque constant (&tau; = N I A B) so scale deflection is linear (&phi; &prop; I).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q26: Why is a soft iron cylinder placed inside a moving coil galvanometer?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Soft iron has very high permeability (&mu;<sub>r</sub> &gt;&gt; 1000), which concentrates magnetic field lines and makes the field strictly radial.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q27: Define current sensitivity of a galvanometer.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Current sensitivity is deflection per unit current: <b>I<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&phi;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span></span> [div A<sup>-1</sup> or rad A<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q28: Define voltage sensitivity of a galvanometer.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Voltage sensitivity is deflection per unit potential difference: <b>V<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&phi;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k R<sub>g</sub></span></span> [div V<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q29: How is a galvanometer converted into an ammeter?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">By connecting a very low resistance called a <b>shunt (S) in parallel</b> across the galvanometer: <b>S = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub> G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I - I<sub>g</sub></span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q30: How is a galvanometer converted into a voltmeter?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">By connecting a <b>high resistance (R) in series</b> with the galvanometer: <b>R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub></span></span> - G</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q31: What is the resistance of an ideal ammeter and ideal voltmeter?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">• Ideal Ammeter: <b>0 &Omega; (Zero)</b>.<br>• Ideal Voltmeter: <b>&infin; (Infinite)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q32: State Gauss's Law for Magnetism in integral form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>&oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>A</b> = 0</b>, stating that total net magnetic flux through any closed Gaussian surface is zero.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q33: What is the fundamental physical significance of Gauss's Law for Magnetism?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">It signifies that <b>isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist in nature</b>; magnetic poles always exist in equal and opposite pairs.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q34: What is the ratio of axial to equatorial magnetic field of a short bar magnet at distance r?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>B<sub>axial</sub> : B<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</b> [B<sub>axial</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span>, B<sub>eq</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>3</sup></span></span>].</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q35: What is the potential energy of a magnetic dipole in stable and unstable equilibrium?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">• Stable Equilibrium (&theta; = 0&deg;): U<sub>min</sub> = - M B.<br>• Unstable Equilibrium (&theta; = 180&deg;): U<sub>max</sub> = + M B.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q36: Define magnetic susceptibility (&chi;<sub>m</sub>) of a substance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Magnetic susceptibility is the ratio of intensity of magnetization (M) induced in a material to applied magnetic intensity (H): <b>&chi;<sub>m</sub> = M / H</b> (dimensionless).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q37: State the relation between relative magnetic permeability (&mu;<sub>r</sub>) and susceptibility (&chi;<sub>m</sub>).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>&mu;<sub>r</sub> = 1 + &chi;<sub>m</sub></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q38: What is the magnetic susceptibility of a diamagnetic material?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">It is <b>negative and small</b> (&chi;<sub>m</sub> &lt; 0, ~ -10<sup>-5</sup>), independent of temperature.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q39: State Curie's Law for paramagnetic substances.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Paramagnetic susceptibility is inversely proportional to absolute temperature: <b>&chi;<sub>m</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T</span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q40: What is Curie Temperature (T<sub>c</sub>)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Curie Temperature is the critical transition temperature above which a <b>ferromagnetic material loses domain alignment and becomes paramagnetic</b> (e.g. Iron T<sub>c</sub> = 1043 K).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00b09b; border-bottom: 2px solid #00b09b; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">SECTION B: Short Answer Questions (2 &amp; 3 Marks Each) — Q41 to Q80</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q41: Derive the formula for magnetic field at the center of a circular current-carrying loop of radius R.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Setup:</b> Loop of radius R carrying current I. Divide circumference into elements d<b>l</b>.<br><b>2. Biot-Savart Law:</b> Distance r = R, angle &theta; = 90&deg; (sin 90&deg; = 1).<br>dB = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [I dl / R<sup>2</sup>].<br><b>3. Integration:</b> B = &int; dB = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; R<sup>2</sup></span></span>) &int; dl = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; R<sup>2</sup></span></span>) (2&pi; R).<br>For N turns: <b>B<sub>center</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 R</span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q42: Using Ampere's circuital law, derive the magnetic field at distance r from an infinitely long straight wire.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Setup:</b> Infinite straight wire carrying current I. Choose circular Amperian loop of radius r concentric with wire.<br><b>2. Symmetry:</b> <b>B</b> is tangential everywhere (angle with d<b>l</b> is 0&deg;) and magnitude B is constant.<br><b>3. Integration:</b> &oint; <b>B</b> &sdot; d<b>l</b> = B &oint; dl = B (2&pi; r).<br><b>4. Applying ACL:</b> B (2&pi; r) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> I &rArr; <b>B = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; r</span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q43: A straight wire carries a current of 35 A. Find the magnitude of magnetic field at a point 20 cm away from the wire.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: I = 35 A, r = 0.20 m.<br>B = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [2 I / r] = (10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; [2 &times; 35 / 0.20] = 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 350 = <b>3.5 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q44: Calculate the magnetic field at the center of a 100-turn circular coil of radius 8.0 cm carrying current 0.40 A.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: N = 100, R = 0.08 m, I = 0.40 A.<br>B = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 R</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 100 &times; 0.40</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &times; 0.08</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">5.0265 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.16</span></span> = <b>3.14 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q45: Derive the orbital radius and revolution period of a charged particle moving perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Radius:</b> Centripetal force equals magnetic force: m v<sup>2</sup> / r = q v B &rArr; <b>r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m v</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span></span></b>.<br><b>2. Time Period:</b> T = 2&pi; r / v = 2&pi; [m v / (q B)] / v &rArr; <b>T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">q B</span></span></b>.<br>Both period T and frequency f = (q B) / (2&pi; m) are independent of speed v.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q46: An alpha particle and a proton enter a uniform magnetic field with equal kinetic energies. Find the ratio of radii of their paths.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Radius r = &radic;(2 m K) / (q B) &prop; &radic;m / q for constant K, B.<br>For proton (p): m<sub>p</sub> = m, q<sub>p</sub> = e.<br>For alpha (&alpha;): m<sub>&alpha;</sub> = 4m, q<sub>&alpha;</sub> = 2e.<br>Ratio: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>&alpha;</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sub>p</sub></span></span> = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;(4m)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2e</span></span>] / [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e</span></span>] = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &radic;m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2e</span></span> &times; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;m</span></span> = 1.<br><b>r<sub>&alpha;</sub> : r<sub>p</sub> = 1 : 1</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q47: Explain the working principle of a velocity selector with a neat force balance equation.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">A velocity selector uses crossed electric field <b>E</b> (upward) and magnetic field <b>B</b> (inward). For a charge +q moving with speed v along +x, electric force is F<sub>e</sub> = q E (upward) and magnetic force is F<sub>m</sub> = q v B (downward). The forces balance when q E = q v B &rArr; <b>v = E / B</b>. Particles with this speed pass straight undeflected.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q48: Derive the expression for the mutual force per unit length between two long parallel current-carrying conductors.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Setup:</b> Parallel wires 1 and 2 separated by distance d carrying currents I<sub>1</sub> and I<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>2. Field by Wire 1:</b> B<sub>1</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub>) / (2&pi; d) (inward).<br><b>3. Force on length L of Wire 2:</b> F = I<sub>2</sub> L B<sub>1</sub> sin 90&deg; = I<sub>2</sub> L [(&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub>) / (2&pi; d)].<br><b>4. Force per unit length:</b> <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; d</span></span> [N m<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q49: Two parallel wires 4.0 cm apart carry currents 8.0 A and 5.0 A in opposite directions. Find force on a 10 cm length of wire.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: d = 0.04 m, I<sub>1</sub> = 8.0 A, I<sub>2</sub> = 5.0 A, L = 0.10 m.<br>F = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; d</span></span>] &times; L = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 8.0 &times; 5.0</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; &times; 0.04</span></span>] &times; 0.10<br>F = [2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 40 / 0.04] &times; 0.10 = <b>2.0 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> N (Repulsive)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q50: Derive the formula for torque on a rectangular current loop ABCD of dimensions a &times; b in a uniform magnetic field B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Forces on Arms:</b> Forces on parallel arms BC and DA are equal and opposite along the same line (&Sigma; F = 0).<br>Forces on arms AB and CD: F = I b B (perpendicular couple separated by arm a sin &theta;).<br><b>2. Torque:</b> &tau; = Force &times; Lever arm = (I b B) (a sin &theta;) = I (a b) B sin &theta;.<br>Since Area A = a &times; b, for N turns: <b>&tau; = N I A B sin &theta; = M B sin &theta; = <b>M</b> &times; <b>B</b></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q51: A 30-turn circular coil of radius 12 cm carries current 6.0 A in a uniform magnetic field 0.50 T. Find maximum torque on coil.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: N = 30, R = 0.12 m, I = 6.0 A, B = 0.50 T.<br>Area A = &pi; R<sup>2</sup> = 3.1416 &times; (0.12)<sup>2</sup> = 0.04524 m<sup>2</sup>.<br>M = N I A = 30 &times; 6.0 &times; 0.04524 = 8.143 A m<sup>2</sup>.<br>&tau;<sub>max</sub> = M B = 8.143 &times; 0.50 = <b>4.07 N m</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q52: Derive the orbital magnetic dipole moment of an electron in hydrogen atom (Bohr Magneton).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Current:</b> Period T = 2&pi; r / v. Current I = e / T = e v / (2&pi; r).<br><b>2. Magnetic Moment:</b> &mu;<sub>l</sub> = I A = [e v / (2&pi; r)] (&pi; r<sup>2</sup>) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e v r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 m<sub>e</sub></span></span> L.<br><b>3. Quantization:</b> Angular momentum L = n &hbar;. For n = 1:<br><b>&mu;<sub>B</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">e &hbar;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 m<sub>e</sub></span></span> = 9.27 &times; 10<sup>-24</sup> A m<sup>2</sup></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q53: Explain why a moving coil galvanometer cannot be used to measure alternating current (AC).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">AC reverses its direction periodically (50 Hz). The deflecting torque reverses every half-cycle, producing zero average torque. Because of coil inertia, the needle cannot track rapid oscillations and remains at zero.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q54: Why does increasing current sensitivity not necessarily increase voltage sensitivity of a galvanometer?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Current sensitivity I<sub>s</sub> = (N A B) / k. Doubling turns N &rarr; 2N doubles I<sub>s</sub>.<br>However, doubling turns doubles wire length and resistance (R<sub>g</sub> &rarr; 2R<sub>g</sub>).<br>Voltage sensitivity V<sub>s</sub> = I<sub>s</sub> / R<sub>g</sub> = (2 I<sub>s</sub>) / (2 R<sub>g</sub>) = I<sub>s</sub> / R<sub>g</sub> remains <b>unchanged</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q55: A galvanometer of resistance 50 &Omega; gives full scale deflection for 2.0 mA. How is it converted into an ammeter of range 0 to 5.0 A?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: G = 50 &Omega;, I<sub>g</sub> = 0.002 A, I = 5.0 A.<br>Shunt S = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub> G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I - I<sub>g</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.002 &times; 50</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">5.0 - 0.002</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.10</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4.998</span></span> = <b>0.020 &Omega; in parallel</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q56: A galvanometer of resistance 100 &Omega; gives full scale deflection for 1.0 mA. How is it converted into a voltmeter of range 0 to 10 V?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Given: G = 100 &Omega;, I<sub>g</sub> = 0.001 A, V = 10 V.<br>Series R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub></span></span> - G = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">10</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.001</span></span> - 100 = 10000 - 100 = <b>9900 &Omega; (9.9 k&Omega;) in series</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q57: State four fundamental properties of magnetic field lines.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">1. Form continuous closed loops without start or end.<br>2. Tangent gives direction of <b>B</b>.<br>3. Never intersect.<br>4. Crowding indicates magnetic field intensity.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q58: Compare the magnetic field of a bar magnet with that of a finite current-carrying solenoid.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">1. Both produce identical dipolar field patterns.<br>2. Outside: lines run N &rarr; S; inside: lines run S &rarr; N.<br>3. Both experience torque &tau; = M B sin &theta;.<br>4. Cutting either in half creates two complete dipoles.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q59: Distinguish between magnetic intensity (H) and intensity of magnetization (M).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">1. <b>Magnetic Intensity (H):</b> External magnetizing field capability: H = B<sub>0</sub> / &mu;<sub>0</sub> [A m<sup>-1</sup>].<br>2. <b>Intensity of Magnetization (M):</b> Net magnetic dipole moment induced per unit volume: M = M<sub>net</sub> / V [A m<sup>-1</sup>].</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q60: Explain diamagnetism on the basis of orbital electron dynamics (Lenz's Law).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">In diamagnetic atoms with paired electrons (zero net intrinsic dipole moment), an applied external field alters electron orbital speeds such that induced magnetic moments oppose the external field (Lenz's Law), causing feeble repulsion.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q61: Explain domain theory of ferromagnetism.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Ferromagnetic materials contain microscopic regions called <b>domains</b> (~1 mm<sup>3</sup> containing ~10<sup>11</sup> atoms) with aligned dipoles due to quantum exchange coupling. An external field rotates and expands aligned domains, producing strong net magnetization.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q62: State Curie-Weiss Law for ferromagnetic substances above Curie temperature.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Above Curie temperature T<sub>c</sub>, ferromagnetic materials become paramagnetic and follow Curie-Weiss Law: <b>&chi;<sub>m</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T - T<sub>c</sub></span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q63: Why is soft iron used for electromagnets while steel is used for permanent magnets?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">• <b>Soft Iron:</b> High permeability, high saturation, low retentivity, and low coercivity (magnetizes and demagnetizes easily with low hysteresis loss).<br>• <b>Steel / Alnico:</b> High retentivity and high coercivity (retains strong magnetization permanently).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q64: A uniform field B = 1.5 T acts on a proton entering at 2.0 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> perpendicular to the field. Find acceleration.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">F = q v B = (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup>) &times; (2.0 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) &times; 1.5 = 4.8 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup> N.<br>a = F / m = (4.8 &times; 10<sup>-12</sup>) / (1.67 &times; 10<sup>-27</sup>) = <b>2.87 &times; 10<sup>15</sup> m s<sup>-2</sup></b> towards center.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q65: Find the pitch of helical path of an electron moving at 3.0 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup> at 60&deg; to field B = 0.20 T.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">v<sub>||</sub> = v cos 60&deg; = 1.5 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>.<br>T = (2&pi; m) / (q B) = (2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 9.11 &times; 10<sup>-31</sup>) / (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> &times; 0.20) = 1.788 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup> s.<br>Pitch p = v<sub>||</sub> T = (1.5 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) &times; (1.788 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup>) = <b>0.268 mm</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q66: A short bar magnet of moment 0.32 J T<sup>-1</sup> is in field 0.15 T. Find work done in turning from stable to unstable equilibrium.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">W = - M B (cos 180&deg; - cos 0&deg;) = 2 M B = 2 &times; 0.32 &times; 0.15 = <b>0.096 Joules = 9.6 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> J</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q67: A magnetic dipole of moment 0.48 J T<sup>-1</sup> is at 30&deg; to a field of 0.22 T. Find torque experienced.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">&tau; = M B sin 30&deg; = 0.48 &times; 0.22 &times; 0.5 = <b>0.0528 N m = 5.28 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> N m</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q68: Why do two magnetic field lines never intersect?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">If two field lines intersected, two tangents could be drawn at that single point, indicating two distinct directions of resultant magnetic field, which is physically impossible.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q69: A solenoid of 500 turns and length 0.5 m carries 2.5 A. Find magnetic field at center and at one end.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">n = 500 / 0.5 = 1000 m<sup>-1</sup>.<br>Center: B<sub>center</sub> = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 1000 &times; 2.5 = <b>3.14 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> T</b>.<br>End: B<sub>end</sub> = 1/2 B<sub>center</sub> = <b>1.57 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> T</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q70: A circular loop of radius 10 cm carries 10 A. Find magnetic field at an axial point 10 cm from center.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">B = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I R<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 10 &times; 0.01</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &times; (0.02)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1.2566 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">5.657 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup></span></span> = <b>2.22 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> T</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q71: Explain the Meissner effect and why superconductors are perfect diamagnets.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Below critical temperature T<sub>c</sub>, superconductors completely expel magnetic flux from their interior (B<sub>internal</sub> = 0). Thus, relative permeability &mu;<sub>r</sub> = 0 and susceptibility &chi;<sub>m</sub> = -1, exhibiting perfect diamagnetism.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q72: A magnetic material has relative permeability &mu;<sub>r</sub> = 400. Find susceptibility &chi;<sub>m</sub> and classify material.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">&chi;<sub>m</sub> = &mu;<sub>r</sub> - 1 = 400 - 1 = <b>399</b>.<br>Since &chi;<sub>m</sub> is positive and very large, the substance is <b>Ferromagnetic</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q73: Find magnetic moment of a solenoid of 1000 turns, area 2.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> m<sup>2</sup> carrying 2.0 A.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">M = N I A = 1000 &times; 2.0 &times; (2.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>) = <b>0.40 A m<sup>2</sup> (or J T<sup>-1</sup>)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q74: An electron moves in a circle of radius 0.53 &Aring; at 2.2 &times; 10<sup>6</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>. Find magnetic field at orbit center.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">I = e v / (2&pi; r) = (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> &times; 2.2 &times; 10<sup>6</sup>) / (2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 0.53 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup>) = 1.057 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> A.<br>B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I) / (2 r) = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 1.057 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup>) / (2 &times; 0.53 &times; 10<sup>-10</sup>) = <b>12.5 Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q75: Two identical coils carry equal currents I in perpendicular planes with common center. Find resultant magnetic field.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Each coil produces B<sub>0</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I) / (2 R). Since fields are perpendicular, resultant is <b>B<sub>net</sub> = &radic;(B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> + B<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup>) = &radic;2 B<sub>0</sub> = ${frac('&mu;<sub>0</sub> I', '&radic;2 R')}</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q76: What is the effect on magnetic susceptibility of a diamagnetic substance when temperature increases from 100 K to 300 K?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>No effect (remains unchanged)</b>, because diamagnetism is an atomic orbital phenomenon independent of thermal agitation.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q77: Calculate the torque required to hold a bar magnet of M = 0.5 J T<sup>-1</sup> at 60&deg; to field B = 0.4 T.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">&tau; = M B sin 60&deg; = 0.5 &times; 0.4 &times; (&radic;3 / 2) = 0.2 &times; 0.866 = <b>0.173 N m</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q78: A current loop of area 5.0 cm<sup>2</sup> carrying 1.5 A is in 0.2 T field. Find maximum potential energy.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">M = I A = 1.5 &times; (5.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>) = 7.5 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> A m<sup>2</sup>.<br>U<sub>max</sub> = + M B = (7.5 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>) &times; 0.2 = <b>+ 1.5 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> J</b> (at &theta; = 180&deg;).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q79: A galvanometer with resistance 20 &Omega; and full-scale current 5 mA is shunted with 0.05 &Omega;. Find new range.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">S = (I<sub>g</sub> G) / (I - I<sub>g</sub>) &rArr; 0.05 = (0.005 &times; 20) / (I - 0.005) &rArr; I - 0.005 = 2.0 &rArr; <b>I = 2.005 A</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q80: Why is an ammeter always in series and a voltmeter always in parallel in circuits?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">• <b>Ammeter (Low R &approx; 0):</b> Connected in <b>series</b> so full branch current flows through it without modifying circuit resistance.<br>• <b>Voltmeter (High R &approx; &infin;):</b> Connected in <b>parallel</b> to measure potential difference without siphoning significant branch current.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n<h2 style=\"color: #00b09b; border-bottom: 2px solid #00b09b; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">SECTION C: Long Answer Derivations &amp; Complex Numericals (5 Marks Each) — Q81 to Q100</h2>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q81: Derive the mathematical expression for the magnetic field at any point on the axis of a circular current loop using Biot-Savart Law.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Geometry:</b> Circular loop of radius R in y-z plane carrying current I. Point P is on x-axis at distance x from center O.<br>Distance to element d<b>l</b>: r = &radic;(R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>). Angle between d<b>l</b> and <b>r̂</b> is 90&deg;.<br><br><b>2. Biot-Savart Law:</b> dB = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [I dl / (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)].<br><br><b>3. Component Resolution:</b> Perpendicular component dB<sub>&perp;</sub> = dB cos &alpha; cancels across opposite elements (&Sigma; dB<sub>&perp;</sub> = 0).<br>Axial component: dB<sub>x</sub> = dB sin &alpha;, where sin &alpha; = R / &radic;(R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>).<br><br><b>4. Integration:</b><br>B = &int; dB sin &alpha; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span> &int; dl = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I R (2&pi; R)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>.<br>For N turns: <b>B<sub>axial</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I R<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span></b>.<br><br><b>5. Special Cases:</b><br>• Center (x = 0): B<sub>center</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I) / (2 R).<br>• Far distance (x &gt;&gt; R): B = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> 2 M) / (4&pi; x<sup>3</sup>), where M = N I (&pi; R<sup>2</sup>).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q82: Derive the expression for force between two infinitely long straight parallel conductors and hence define standard SI Ampere.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Setup:</b> Parallel wires 1 and 2 separated by distance d carrying currents I<sub>1</sub> and I<sub>2</sub> in vacuum.<br><br><b>2. Field by Wire 1 at Wire 2:</b> B<sub>1</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub>) / (2&pi; d) (perpendicularly inward).<br><br><b>3. Force on Wire 2:</b> Length L experiences F<sub>21</sub> = I<sub>2</sub> L B<sub>1</sub> sin 90&deg; = I<sub>2</sub> L [(&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub>) / (2&pi; d)].<br><br><b>4. Force per unit length:</b> <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">F</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">L</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>1</sub> I<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2&pi; d</span></span> [N m<sup>-1</sup>]</b>.<br><br><b>5. Standard Definition:</b> When I<sub>1</sub> = I<sub>2</sub> = 1 A and d = 1 m in vacuum:<br>F/L = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 1 &times; 1) / (2&pi; &times; 1) = <b>2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> N m<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>1 Ampere is that steady current producing mutual force of 2 &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> N m<sup>-1</sup> between infinite parallel wires 1 m apart.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q83: Describe the construction, theory, and working of a Moving Coil Galvanometer. Explain how radial magnetic field is achieved.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Construction:</b> Rectangular coil of insulated copper wire suspended between cylindrical concave magnetic poles N and S with a soft iron cylindrical core. Phosphor-bronze strip and hairspring provide restoring torque k.<br><br><b>2. Radial Magnetic Field:</b> Concave poles and cylindrical soft iron core direct field lines radially. Thus, the coil plane is always parallel to <b>B</b> (&theta; = 90&deg;), maintaining constant maximum torque &tau;<sub>d</sub> = N I A B.<br><br><b>3. Equilibrium Working:</b><br>&tau;<sub>d</sub> = &tau;<sub>r</sub> &rArr; N I A B = k &phi; &rArr; <b>I = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">k</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">N A B</span></span>] &phi; = G<sub>0</sub> &phi;</b>.<br>Since k, N, A, B are fixed constants, deflection is directly proportional to current (<b>&phi; &prop; I</b>).</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q84: Derive the mathematical formulas for converting a galvanometer into: (a) an ammeter of range 0 to I, (b) a voltmeter of range 0 to V.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>(a) Ammeter Conversion:</b><br>• Connect low resistance shunt S in parallel with galvanometer G.<br>• Branch currents: I<sub>g</sub> through G, (I - I<sub>g</sub>) through S.<br>• Equal potential: I<sub>g</sub> G = (I - I<sub>g</sub>) S &rArr; <b>S = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub> G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I - I<sub>g</sub></span></span></b>.<br>Total Ammeter Resistance: <b>R<sub>A</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G S</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G + S</span></span> &approx; 0</b>.<br><br><b>(b) Voltmeter Conversion:</b><br>• Connect high resistance R in series with galvanometer G.<br>• Total voltage: V = I<sub>g</sub> (G + R) &rArr; <b>R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">V</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub></span></span> - G</b>.<br>Total Voltmeter Resistance: <b>R<sub>V</sub> = G + R &approx; &infin;</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q85: Show that a planar current loop of area A carrying current I behaves as a magnetic dipole. Find axial and equatorial fields.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Axial Field:</b> B<sub>axial</sub> = [(&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I R<sup>2</sup>) / (2 (R<sup>2</sup> + x<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup>)].<br>For x &gt;&gt; R: B<sub>axial</sub> &approx; (&mu;<sub>0</sub> N I R<sup>2</sup>) / (2 x<sup>3</sup>) = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [2 (N I &pi; R<sup>2</sup>) / x<sup>3</sup>].<br>Defining magnetic dipole moment <b>M = N I A</b> gives: <b>B<sub>axial</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> 2 M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; x<sup>3</sup></span></span></b>.<br><br><b>2. Equatorial Field:</b> At equatorial distance r &gt;&gt; R: <b>B<sub>eq</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b> (directed opposite to <b>M</b>).<br>Ratio: <b>B<sub>axial</sub> / B<sub>eq</sub> = 2 : 1</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q86: Compare Diamagnetic, Paramagnetic, and Ferromagnetic substances across 6 distinct physical properties with examples.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. External Field Action:</b> Dia feebly repelled; Para feebly attracted; Ferro strongly concentrated into strong fields.<br><b>2. Susceptibility (&chi;<sub>m</sub>):</b> Dia: Negative &amp; small (-10<sup>-5</sup>); Para: Positive &amp; small (+10<sup>-4</sup>); Ferro: Positive &amp; very large (&gt;&gt; 1000).<br><b>3. Relative Permeability (&mu;<sub>r</sub>):</b> Dia: 0 &le; &mu;<sub>r</sub> &lt; 1; Para: &mu;<sub>r</sub> &gt; 1; Ferro: &mu;<sub>r</sub> &gt;&gt; 1000.<br><b>4. Temperature Dependence:</b> Dia: Independent of T; Para: Follows Curie's Law (&chi; &prop; 1/T); Ferro: Follows Curie-Weiss Law above T<sub>c</sub> (&chi; = C/(T - T<sub>c</sub>)).<br><b>5. Hysteresis:</b> Dia &amp; Para show zero hysteresis; Ferro exhibits prominent B-H hysteresis loop.<br><b>6. Examples:</b> Dia: Bi, Cu, Water; Para: Al, Na, Liquid O<sub>2</sub>; Ferro: Fe, Co, Ni.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q87: Derive torque &tau; = M &times; B and potential energy U = -M &middot; B for a magnetic dipole in uniform magnetic field. Discuss stability.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Torque:</b> Couple on poles of strength m and separation 2l: &tau; = (m B) (2l sin &theta;) = <b>M B sin &theta; &rArr; <b>&tau;</b> = <b>M</b> &times; <b>B</b></b>.<br><br><b>2. Potential Energy:</b> dW = &tau; d&theta; = M B sin &theta; d&theta;.<br>U = &int;<sub>90&deg;</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> M B sin &theta; d&theta; = - M B [cos &theta;]<sub>90&deg;</sub><sup>&theta;</sup> = <b>- M B cos &theta; = - <b>M</b> &sdot; <b>B</b></b>.<br><br><b>3. Stability:</b><br>• Stable (&theta; = 0&deg;): &tau; = 0, U<sub>min</sub> = - M B.<br>• Unstable (&theta; = 180&deg;): &tau; = 0, U<sub>max</sub> = + M B.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q88: A square loop of side 10 cm carries 12 A. Find magnetic field at center of the square loop.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Geometry:</b> Square side a = 0.10 m. Distance to center d = a / 2 = 0.05 m. Subtended angles: &theta;<sub>1</sub> = &theta;<sub>2</sub> = 45&deg;.<br><br><b>2. Field by One Side:</b><br>B<sub>1</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span></span>] (sin 45&deg; + sin 45&deg;) = (10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">12</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.05</span></span>] &times; [2 / &radic;2] = 3.394 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> T.<br><br><b>3. Total Field by 4 Sides:</b> <b>B<sub>total</sub> = 4 &times; B<sub>1</sub> = 4 &times; 3.394 &times; 10<sup>-5</sup> = 1.36 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q89: A galvanometer coil has resistance 15 &Omega; and meter shows full scale deflection for 4 mA. Calculate shunt needed for 0 to 6 A range and effective resistance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Shunt Resistance S:</b><br>S = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I<sub>g</sub> G</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">I - I<sub>g</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.004 &times; 15</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">6.0 - 0.004</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">0.060</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">5.996</span></span> = <b>0.010 &Omega; (1.0 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> &Omega;)</b>.<br><br><b>2. Effective Resistance:</b><br>R<sub>A</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G S</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">G + S</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">15 &times; 0.010</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">15.01</span></span> = <b>0.010 &Omega;</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q90: Two circular concentric coils X and Y of radii 16 cm and 10 cm carry currents 0.75 A and 1.0 A in mutually perpendicular planes. Find resultant field at center.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Coil X:</b> B<sub>x</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>x</sub>) / (2 R<sub>x</sub>) = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 0.75) / (2 &times; 0.16) = 2.945 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> T.<br><b>2. Coil Y:</b> B<sub>y</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I<sub>y</sub>) / (2 R<sub>y</sub>) = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup> &times; 1.0) / (2 &times; 0.10) = 6.283 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> T.<br><b>3. Resultant Field:</b> <b>B<sub>net</sub> = &radic;(B<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup> + B<sub>y</sub><sup>2</sup>) = &radic;((2.945)<sup>2</sup> + (6.283)<sup>2</sup>) &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> = 6.94 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q91: An electron accelerated through 10 kV enters uniform field B = 0.04 T perpendicular to path. Find orbit radius and revolution frequency.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Velocity:</b> 1/2 m v<sup>2</sup> = e V &rArr; v = &radic;(2 e V / m) = &radic;[(2 &times; 1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> &times; 10<sup>4</sup>) / (9.11 &times; 10<sup>-31</sup>)] = 5.93 &times; 10<sup>7</sup> m s<sup>-1</sup>.<br><b>2. Radius:</b> r = (m v) / (e B) = (9.11 &times; 10<sup>-31</sup> &times; 5.93 &times; 10<sup>7</sup>) / (1.6 &times; 10<sup>-19</sup> &times; 0.04) = <b>8.44 mm</b>.<br><b>3. Frequency:</b> f = (e B) / (2&pi; m) = <b>1.12 GHz (1.12 &times; 10<sup>9</sup> Hz)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q92: A solenoid of length 0.6 m and radius 2.0 cm has 3 layers of 500 turns each. If current is 5 A, find total flux through solenoid.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Turns:</b> N = 3 &times; 500 = 1500 turns. n = 1500 / 0.6 = 2500 m<sup>-1</sup>.<br><b>2. Field:</b> B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 2500 &times; 5.0 = 0.0157 T.<br><b>3. Total Flux:</b> &Phi;<sub>total</sub> = N (B A) = 1500 &times; [0.0157 &times; &pi; (0.02)<sup>2</sup>] = <b>2.96 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> Weber (Wb)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q93: A uniform wire of length L is bent first into a 1-turn circular coil, then into a 2-turn coil. Compare center magnetic fields for same current I.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Single Turn (N<sub>1</sub> = 1):</b> R<sub>1</sub> = L / (2&pi;). B<sub>1</sub> = (&mu;<sub>0</sub> I) / (2 R<sub>1</sub>) = &mu;<sub>0</sub> &pi; I / L.<br><b>2. Two Turns (N<sub>2</sub> = 2):</b> R<sub>2</sub> = L / (4&pi;) = R<sub>1</sub> / 2. B<sub>2</sub> = [&mu;<sub>0</sub> (2) I] / [2 (R<sub>1</sub> / 2)] = 4 B<sub>1</sub>.<br><b>Ratio: B<sub>2</sub> : B<sub>1</sub> = 4 : 1</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q94: A toroid of non-ferromagnetic core with inner radius 25 cm and outer radius 26 cm has 3500 turns. For current 11 A, find B inside core.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Mean Radius:</b> r = (25 + 26) / 2 = 25.5 cm = 0.255 m.<br><b>2. Turns per meter:</b> n = 3500 / (2&pi; &times; 0.255) = 2184.7 m<sup>-1</sup>.<br><b>3. Field:</b> B = &mu;<sub>0</sub> n I = (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>) &times; 2184.7 &times; 11 = <b>3.02 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q95: A circular coil of 20 turns and radius 10 cm is placed in uniform field 0.10 T normal to coil. Calculate total torque and total force.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Coil normal to field &rArr; &theta; = 0&deg;.<br><b>1. Force:</b> Net translational force on any closed current loop in uniform field is strictly <b>Zero (F<sub>net</sub> = 0)</b>.<br><b>2. Torque:</b> &tau; = N I A B sin 0&deg; = <b>0 N m</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q96: A galvanometer with resistance 12 &Omega; has 30 divisions and current sensitivity 20 &mu;A per division. Find resistance needed for 6.0 V voltmeter.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Full Scale Current:</b> I<sub>g</sub> = 30 &times; (20 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>) = 6.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> A.<br><b>2. Series Resistor:</b> R = (V / I<sub>g</sub>) - G = (6.0 / (6.0 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup>)) - 12 = 10000 - 12 = <b>9988 &Omega; (9.99 k&Omega;)</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q97: Derive magnetic field of a bar magnet at axial and equatorial points.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Axial Line:</b> B<sub>axial</sub> = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(r - l)<sup>2</sup></span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(r + l)<sup>2</sup></span></span>] = (<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi;</span></span>) [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">m (4 r l)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(r<sup>2</sup> - l<sup>2</sup>)<sup>2</sup></span></span>].<br>For short magnet (r &gt;&gt; l): <b>B<sub>axial</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> 2 M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b>.<br><br><b>2. Equatorial Line:</b> B<sub>eq</sub> = 2 B<sub>pole</sub> cos &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; (r<sup>2</sup> + l<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3/2</sup></span></span>.<br>For short magnet: <b>B<sub>eq</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&mu;<sub>0</sub> M</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4&pi; r<sup>3</sup></span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q98: A magnetic needle free to rotate in horizontal plane has moment 6.7 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup> A m<sup>2</sup> and moment of inertia 7.5 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup> kg m<sup>2</sup>. It performs 10 oscillations in 6.70 s. Find horizontal field B<sub>H</sub>.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Period:</b> T = 6.70 / 10 = 0.67 s.<br><b>2. Formula:</b> T = 2&pi; &radic;(I<sub>inertia</sub> / (M B<sub>H</sub>)) &rArr; B<sub>H</sub> = (4&pi;<sup>2</sup> I<sub>inertia</sub>) / (M T<sup>2</sup>).<br><b>3. Value:</b> B<sub>H</sub> = [4 &times; (3.1416)<sup>2</sup> &times; (7.5 &times; 10<sup>-6</sup>)] / [(6.7 &times; 10<sup>-2</sup>) &times; (0.67)<sup>2</sup>] = <b>9.84 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> Tesla &approx; 0.010 Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q99: Explain the origin of Paramagnetism and derive Curie's Law from Boltzmann statistical thermal distribution.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Origin:</b> Paramagnetic atoms possess permanent magnetic dipole moments due to unpaired electrons.<br><b>2. Thermal Randomization:</b> Without external field, thermal energy k<sub>B</sub> T disorients dipoles, yielding zero net magnetization (M = 0).<br><b>3. Field Alignment:</b> External field <b>B</b> exerts torque &tau; = M B sin &theta; aligning dipoles.<br><b>4. Curie Law:</b> Aligned fraction &prop; (M B) / (k<sub>B</sub> T) &rArr; M = C (B / T) &rArr; &chi;<sub>m</sub> = M / H = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">C</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T</span></span></b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.75); border: 1.2px solid rgba(0, 176, 155, 0.25); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 18px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #00b09b; margin: 0 0 12px 0; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q100: A Rowland ring of mean radius 15 cm and 3500 turns of wire is wound on a ferromagnetic core of relative permeability 800. Find magnetic field B in core for current 1.2 A.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border-left: 3.5px solid #00b09b; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #00b09b; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">Answer:</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\"><b>1. Circumference:</b> L = 2&pi; (0.15) = 0.9425 m. n = 3500 / 0.9425 = 3713.5 m<sup>-1</sup>.<br><b>2. Magnetic Intensity H:</b> H = n I = 3713.5 &times; 1.2 = 4456.2 A m<sup>-1</sup>.<br><b>3. Field B:</b> B = &mu; H = (&mu;<sub>r</sub> &mu;<sub>0</sub>) H = [800 &times; (4&pi; &times; 10<sup>-7</sup>)] &times; 4456.2 = <b>4.48 Tesla</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</div>";

